10.3389/fpsyg.2025.1520046.abstract:
Cross-modal correspondences between audition and olfaction have received relatively less attention compared to other modality pairs. This study expands on previous work regarding timbre-aroma correspondences by examining the semantic mediation hypothesis, according to which cross-modal correspondences may be partly explained by the existence of common semantic qualities. In a behavioral experiment, 26 musically trained participants rated 26 complex synthetic tones and 12 aromatic stimuli across two separate blocks using a common set of semantic scales. The analysis of semantic variables identified a largely consistent organization for both modalities, condensing into three prominent clusters: [
                    bright, fresh, ethereal
                    ], [
                    sharp, metallic
                    ], and [
                    full, rich, warm
                    ]. Furthermore, distances between stimuli derived from semantic ratings and optimized through a genetic algorithm exhibited a strong correlation with previously estimated ground-truth distances of direct cross-modal associations. Additionally, the stimulus configuration within the semantic space generated through Multidimensional Scaling analysis exhibited notable commonalities with the organization of stimuli derived from direct timbre-aroma correspondences. Overall, this study provides compelling evidence that semantic mediation plays a significant role in shaping cross-modal correspondences between auditory and olfactory stimuli, paving the way for further exploration of the underlying semantic dimensions that connect these two modalities.

10.1177/21582440261431430.abstract:
Nouns and verbs are basic word classes in any language. Based on the 11 lexical variables (age of acquisition, concreteness, imageability, sensory experience arousal, emotion arousal, valence, familiarity, semantic transparency, number of strokes, number of meanings, and frequency) of Chinese two-character words from an existing dataset, the present study enriched the data by conducting a lexical decision task and a word class rating task (available at
                    https://osf.io/x86bs/?view_only=0c0f5c3ae7ab423baa76d50bcb597cd2
                    ). We compared nouns with verbs in patterns of variable score distribution, variable correlation, and prediction of variables on lexical processing in lexical decision task. The results showed that nouns had higher scores in concreteness, imageability, sensory experience arousal, number of meanings, and valence and obtained lower scores in age of acquisition, frequency, emotion arousal, number of strokes, familiarity, and semantic transparency than verbs. Nouns were both similar to and different from verbs in patterns of feature correlations and feature prediction during lexical access. Skilled readers might be more sensitive to imageability, valence, and emotion arousal but less conscious of frequency of nouns than of verbs. Semantic variables accounted for a greater share of processing variances for nouns than for verbs in lexical decision task. Those results addressed the primary role of semantics in distinguishing nouns from verbs in the absence of syntactic demands. The broader implication is that semantics and grammar might co-evolve and jointly contribute to noun-verb distinctions, which still requires further verification in tasks with explicit syntactic demands. These findings further our understanding on Chinese word class.
